# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: branch diff against origin/main
Verdict: needs-attention

Do not accept yet: startup and shutdown synchronization remain underspecified, and the promised non-finite-command halt conflicts with configurable input health behavior. Reviewed e16d26a..d058a3a; no edits or builds.

Findings:
- [high] Gate attachment on segment size and observed SIM_READY (docs/decisions/0024-shm-layout-header-and-protocol.md:39-44)
  The segment becomes discoverable before sizing and initialization finish. Retrying open alone therefore permits attachment to a zero-length segment, where header access can fault, or to an unfinished header. An acquire-load only publishes initialized contents if it actually observes SIM_READY; the specified sequence does not require waiting for that value before validation. Following it can fail valid delayed-start runs, contrary to R5.
  Recommendation: Require checking the backing size before header access, waiting until an acquire-load observes SIM_READY before validating initialized fields, and using one handshake deadline throughout. Specify tests pausing the creator before sizing and before publishing readiness.
- [high] Make the non-finite-command halt independent of input health configuration (docs/decisions/0022-lockstep-exchange-stage.md:56-58)
  The claimed halt delegates to ADR-0017's health.input, which permits warn/off. Architecture §15 explicitly preserves last-good input values under warn, and HealthMonitor::check_input applies input_action_ (health_monitor.cpp:151–154). Thus a NaN elevator reply routed through the prescribed seam can retain the previous command and continue instead of satisfying R5's mandatory halt. ADR-0022 neither restricts this configuration nor defines an unconditional input-fault path.
  Recommendation: Define how transport-enabled runs guarantee a named input halt: reject warn/off for health.input at initialization, or require an unconditional transport-command finite check retaining the input diagnostic. Preserve existing behavior when transport is absent and cover warn/off explicitly in acceptance.
- [medium] Close the lost-wakeup window between CLOSED and req_seq waits (docs/decisions/0024-shm-layout-header-and-protocol.md:51-52)
  The HAL waits on req_seq, but shutdown changes only sim_state. If the HAL checks sim_state, the sim publishes CLOSED and wakes, and the HAL then enters FUTEX_WAIT on the unchanged req_seq, the wake is lost. The documented protocol can consequently wait until timeout on normal completion; ADR-0023 specifies nonzero exit on expiry, conflicting with the required clean exit. No timeout-versus-CLOSED precedence or race-safe shutdown notification is specified.
  Recommendation: Specify a shutdown notification that changes the predicate watched by the futex, or explicitly require a CLOSED recheck before classifying timeout and accept the bounded shutdown delay. Add a test forcing closure between the HAL's state check and futex entry.

Next steps:
- Resolve these contracts in the proposed ADRs and matching architecture text before acceptance.
